{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Scope note: capping journald is deliberately NOT part of this repair\n\nMoved out of the description to stay under the plan-review admission limit; recorded here so the\nreasoning is not lost.\n\nEpic 6202 story S3 (`divine-streaky-betafish`, Gerrit change 2643) measured the host's journal at\n1.7 GB against an implicit `SystemMaxUse` ceiling of `min(10% of filesystem, 4 GiB)` = 4 GiB, which\nwas never exceeded. It already owns adding an explicit cap.\n\nA cap would not have prevented this outage. Twelve unbounded scans of a 3 GiB journal still read\n36 GiB; the journal was inside its ceiling the entire time. The defect is not \"an unbounded journal\ngrew until scans became slow\" — it is that twelve unbounded SCANS of a correctly-sized journal\ncannot complete inside the period once the page cache is cold.\n\nRecording a journald cap as the fix for this defect would therefore be wrong, and `infra/terraform/\nuser_data.sh` executes only at instance launch, so a change there could not repair the running host\nin any case. That file is consequently out of this change's file impact.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788566120349578206,"uuid":"0f61405f-aaac-4dd4-8e4f-84d9da942836"}